+description: |
+  Cognitive Function Analysis (CFA) is the cognitive-engineering method that Guy André Boy
+  formalised at EURISCO, the Airbus–Thales research institute in Toulouse, from the
+  automation studies of the 1980s and 1990s. A cognitive function transforms a task into an
+  activity and is described by its role, its context of validity and the resources it needs;
+  functions are allocated to and redistributed between humans and machines, and the analysis
+  follows them through human-in-the-loop simulation rather than fixing them once at design
+  time. It is the European counterpart of the Fitts-list tradition, with the difference that
+  allocation is treated as dynamic and context-dependent.
+
+  The Orchestra model (Orchestrating Human-Centered Design, 2013) extends it to
+  multi-agent safety-critical systems: composers write the "score" (procedures, rules),
+  conductors coordinate, and human and artificial agents play their parts with an
+  authority that is negotiated rather than fixed — the vocabulary later used for
+  human–AI teaming and for air-traffic authority sharing. Together with the notion of
+  tangible interactive systems (2016), CFA is the root of the human systems integration
+  framework carried by the FlexTech chair.
+provenance:
