+      label: Handbook of Sociotechnical Systems — a HSI approach (CRC Press); Systemic Cognition — on the complexity of context (Systems 14(7))
+description: |
+  Human systems integration (HSI), as framed by Guy André Boy, is the discipline that
+  integrates technology, organizations and people (the TOP model) over the whole life cycle
+  of a sociotechnical system — design, development, validation, operation, maintenance and
+  dismantling. It grew out of cognitive engineering and human-centered design and was made
+  a full discipline through the INCOSE HSI Working Group (chaired by Boy 2014–2024) and the
+  FlexTech chair. Its working concepts are flexibility rather than rigid automation,
+  tangibility (a virtual model must become physically and cognitively graspable),
+  sociotechnical maturity, and the distinction between procedural and declarative
+  knowledge; the PRODEC method (2022) turns them into an agile, scenario-based design
+  loop with human-in-the-loop simulation and digital twins.
+
+  Two recent papers anchor the framework for human–AI collaboration. The 2023
+  epistemological approach (Technology in Society) argues for a consistent terminology
+  and ontology of HSI — complexity, context, procedural versus declarative knowledge,
+  maturity. Systemic cognition (Systems, 2026) puts context at the centre: an STS is
+  understood incrementally through conceptual models built in context, so that emerging
+  behaviours discovered in operation are integrated back into the system. For a map of
+  human–AI decision-making in critical systems, HSI is the systems-engineering frame in
+  which oversight, mixed initiative and human–AI teaming are negotiated as function
+  allocation and authority, not bolted on as interfaces.
