+++ b/data/i18n/descriptions.en.yml
@@ -444,3 +444,75 @@ wmu: Researches the human element of autonomous ships — operator skills, monit
 xmanai: 'XMANAI (H2020 ICT-38-2020, grant 957362, Nov 2020–Apr 2024, EUR 6.0 M, coordinated by TXT e-tech in Milan with Fraunhofer FOKUS as scientific coordinator) built a ''glass-box'' explainable-AI platform for manufacturing, where hybrid and graph-based models are paired with explanation methods so that planners and quality engineers can understand and challenge AI recommendations before acting on them. Explanations were designed around the decision each human role has to take (demand forecasting, production planning, quality and process anomalies) and evaluated with operators in four plants: Ford Valencia, CNH Industrial, Whirlpool/Beko and Unimetrik, with Deep Blue and Politecnico di Milano covering human factors and trust. The 18-partner consortium also produced an XAI catalogue and reference architecture reused by later AI-for-manufacturing projects.'
 york-cfaa: 'The Centre for Assuring Autonomy (CfAA) at the University of York is a GBP 10 million partnership with Lloyd''s Register Foundation, launched in January 2024 to succeed the Assuring Autonomy International Programme (AAIP, 2018–2023, GBP 12 million). Directed by Prof. John McDermid, it produces practical safety-assurance guidance for AI and autonomous systems and validates it on real deployments across automotive, health and social care, maritime, agriculture, manufacturing and aviation. It is the source of AMLAS (Assurance of Machine Learning for Autonomous Systems) and SACE (Safety Assurance of Autonomous Systems in Complex Environments), both already on this map as frameworks. Its human-AI angle is explicit: assurance cases have to state what the human operator is expected to do, what monitoring and override they retain, and whether that expectation is realistic given human-factors evidence.'
 zhaw-cai: The Centre for Artificial Intelligence of ZHAW (Winterthur) works on trustworthy AI and decision support, and is a partner of AI4RealNet, where it contributes to explainable and human-centred AI for critical network operation.
+academie-air-espace: 'The Académie de l''air et de l''espace, founded in Toulouse in 1983, is the French learned society of aeronautics and space. Among its working themes the relation between crews, automation and now artificial intelligence recurs: Dossier 37 (2013) on dealing with unforeseen situations in flight, drawn from a colloquium with airline pilots on facing the unexpected; Dossier 42 (2018) on a more automated, interconnected aviation by 2050 and its companion study on transport-aircraft automation; and an international colloquium in November 2025 on AI applications in aeronautics, space and defence, whose synthesis stresses that the human–machine interface problems predate AI and multiply with it.
+
+
+  Guy André Boy has been a fellow of the academy since 2006 and one of its voices on human systems integration and cognitive engineering — the 2020 note "IA-t-il un pilote dans l''avion ?" and his contribution to the 2025 colloquium carry the HSI position into the French aerospace establishment. The academy sits in the ecosystem tier: it does not run research, but it frames how the aerospace community in Toulouse talks about authority, automation and AI in the cockpit.'
+afis-ct-facteurs-humains: 'AFIS, the Association Française d''Ingénierie Système, has been INCOSE''s French chapter since 1999 and gathers about a thousand systems engineers from industry, research and public agencies. Its Comité technique Facteurs Humains is the place where human factors and systems engineering are worked together in France: its declared aim is to make the human-centred approach and human–system integration (or human–machine teaming) a recognised systems-engineering practice, through method sheets, webinars and working sessions. It is co-led by practitioners from Airbus Defence and Space and Thales — the same industrial base that funded EURISCO and the FlexTech chair.
+
+
+  The committee relays Guy André Boy''s work to the French systems-engineering community: AFIS webinars presented his book Human–Systems Integration — From Virtual to Tangible and model-based human systems integration of human–AI teams. On this map it is the ecosystem-tier link between the INCOSE HSI working group and the French industrial practitioners who apply HSI to cockpits, mission systems and control rooms.'
+airbus-ds-hsi: 'Airbus Defence and Space''s human factors engineering department in Toulouse develops the human–machine interfaces of military air platforms, mission consoles and ground-control stations, and has been one of the few European industrial groups to publish how human systems integration is wired into the systems-engineering life cycle: a 2015 AHFE paper on implementing HSI and "non-functional characteristics" into the life cycle, followed in 2016 by its extension to model-based systems engineering, drawing on the EU FP7 HoliDes project on adaptive cooperative human–machine systems.
+
+
+  Its human-factors lead co-animates the human-factors technical committee of AFIS, the French INCOSE chapter, which promotes human–system integration and human–machine teaming as systems-engineering practice — the institutional channel through which Guy André Boy''s HSI framework reaches French defence programmes. On this map it stands for the industrial application of HSI to mission systems where a crew supervises increasingly autonomous functions.'
+ceam-human-factors: 'The Centre d''expertise aérienne militaire (CEAM), the French Air and Space Force''s Air Warfare Center at Mont-de-Marsan, runs an ergonomics and human factors unit that tests and specifies how aircrews will work with increasingly autonomous systems. Through the FlexTech chair it sponsored two doctoral projects: one on the future combat cloud and the interaction between pilots and virtual assistants, the other on the human-centred adaptation of hybrid (live, virtual, constructive) simulations enriched with AI for Air Force training. The centre also hosts "AI meetings" and engineering students working on AI for military aeronautics.
+
+
+  On this map it is the operational end-user of Guy André Boy''s HSI framework in defence: collaborative air combat where a human pilot remains in command of AI-assisted functions, designed and evaluated with human-in-the-loop simulation. The entry is kept at medium confidence — the unit''s own creation date and the theses'' titles were not published in full.'
+centralesupelec-lgi: 'The Laboratoire Génie Industriel of CentraleSupélec, on the Paris-Saclay campus, works on models, methods and tools for the whole life cycle of sociotechnical systems — design engineering, decision support for product-service systems, safety and risk, sustainable economy. From 2019 to 2024 it hosted, with ESTIA, the FlexTech chair on human systems integration of increasingly autonomous systems, held by Guy André Boy and co-led by the laboratory''s director Bernard Yannou: the chair''s PhD students were registered at Université Paris-Saclay in industrial engineering, and Boy''s papers of the period carry the LGI affiliation.
+
+
+  For the map it is the Île-de-France anchor of the HSI community: the place where the PRODEC method, the Reality Anchor digital-twin methodology and the human-in-the-loop simulation approach were supervised, and where the HSI module of the Paris-Saclay master in system science and design was built.'
+cognitive-function-analysis: 'Cognitive Function Analysis (CFA) is the cognitive-engineering method that Guy André Boy formalised at EURISCO, the Airbus–Thales research institute in Toulouse, from the automation studies of the 1980s and 1990s. A cognitive function transforms a task into an activity and is described by its role, its context of validity and the resources it needs; functions are allocated to and redistributed between humans and machines, and the analysis follows them through human-in-the-loop simulation rather than fixing them once at design time. It is the European counterpart of the Fitts-list tradition, with the difference that allocation is treated as dynamic and context-dependent.
+
+
+  The Orchestra model (Orchestrating Human-Centered Design, 2013) extends it to multi-agent safety-critical systems: composers write the "score" (procedures, rules), conductors coordinate, and human and artificial agents play their parts with an authority that is negotiated rather than fixed — the vocabulary later used for human–AI teaming and for air-traffic authority sharing. Together with the notion of tangible interactive systems (2016), CFA is the root of the human systems integration framework carried by the FlexTech chair.'
+ensc-bordeaux: 'ENSC is the French engineering school of "cognitique" — applied cognitive science and cognitive engineering — created in Bordeaux in 2003 as the Institut de cognitique and part of Bordeaux INP since 2009. It trains engineers with a dual competence in information technology and human sciences, with human factors, human/system integration and the cognitive uses of technology as its core, and its research is carried by the IMS laboratory (UMR CNRS 5218). Since 2009 it has run HEAL, the Human Engineering for Aerospace Lab, a joint laboratory with Thales Avionics and Thales Systèmes Aéroportés on crew workload, anticipation and situation awareness in cockpits.
+
+
+  On this map it is the Bordeaux INP partner of the Horizon Europe project HAIKU (2022–2025), which built and validated six human-centred AI assistants for cockpits, towers and airports, and the institutional home of the French cognitive-engineering school: the Institut de cognitique was founded in 2003 by Bernard Claverie with Guy André Boy, whose Ingénierie cognitive appeared the same year, and cognitive function analysis is part of its teaching lineage. Jean-Marc Salotti''s work on degraded situation awareness and on the human factors of cobotic systems extends the same line to space and manufacturing.'
+estia-recherche: 'ESTIA-Recherche is the research unit of ESTIA, the engineering school of the Basque Country technopole at Bidart, attached to the Université de Bordeaux and a member of Groupe ISAE. Its positioning — durable and empowering interfaces for human–human, human–system and system–system interaction — made it the natural European home of Guy André Boy''s human systems integration work when the FlexTech chair was created with CentraleSupélec in 2019: about twenty researchers across the two sites, CIFRE theses with TotalEnergies (a digital twin designed to support situation awareness in remote operations, the Reality Anchor methodology), Safran Helicopter Engines (SOMA, a multi-agent digital twin for engine maintenance), CS Group, the French Air and Space Force (collaborative air combat, virtual assistants) and SNCF, and the PRODEC scenario-based design method.
+
+
+  The unit also carries the tangible-interaction group (Nadine Couture) and Dimitri Masson''s human-centred framework for digital-twin design, so that "tangibility" — a virtual model becoming physically and cognitively graspable by operators — is worked on as a design property rather than a metaphor. ESTIA hosted HSI2019, the first INCOSE international conference on human systems integration, in Biarritz, and the 2024 spring school on human–AI teaming; since 2025 it leads the FlexTech-2 chair with IRIT in Toulouse.'
+eth-wop-grote: 'Gudela Grote''s chair of Work and Organizational Psychology at ETH Zurich studies how organisations and teams in high-risk systems manage uncertainty, how standardisation and flexibility trade off, and — increasingly — how AI reshapes work processes and accountability. Its empirical ground is railway operations (Swiss Federal Railways), air-traffic control and healthcare, with field studies feeding automation requirements: the 2016 study of en-route controllers showed that automation must support rather than remove the controllers'' own strategies for handling uncertainty.
+
+
+  The chair is the Swiss anchor of Guy André Boy''s network: Boy and Grote co-authored the work on authority in increasingly complex human–machine collaborative systems, applied to the evolution of air-traffic management (IEA 2009) and the "authority issue in organizational automation" chapter of Boy''s 2011 handbook, ETH hosted the announcement of the first INCOSE HSI conference, and Grote taught at the FlexTech spring school on human–AI teaming in 2024. On this map it represents the organisational side of human systems integration — who holds authority when a system becomes more autonomous.'
+eurisco: 'EURISCO (Institut européen de sciences et d''ingénierie cognitives) was the joint cognitive-engineering research institute of Airbus and Thomson-CSF/Thales, founded in Toulouse in 1992 and run until 2008 by Guy André Boy, who had come from ONERA''s cognitive ergonomics group and NASA Ames, with the test pilot Jean Pinet as co-founder and president in the 1990s. Its multidisciplinary staff — cognitive and social scientists, computer scientists, engineers, human-factors specialists — worked with a network of universities and engineering schools, and the institute traded as EURISCO International in its last years. It brought cognitive science into cockpit and air-traffic automation design at the moment glass cockpits and fly-by-wire raised the question of authority between crew and automation: cognitive function analysis, situation awareness and workload in highly automated cockpits, human-centred automation, procedure design and, later, authority sharing in future air-traffic management. It is the industrial origin of cognitive function analysis and of the Orchestra model, and it ran the HCI-Aero conference series with ACM SIGCHI.
+
+
+  The institute closed in 2008; its line of work moved with Boy to the Human-Centered Design Institute at Florida Tech and returned to Europe with the FlexTech chair in 2019. It is kept on the map as a concluded entry so that the period filter shows where the French cognitive-engineering community of the 1990s and 2000s was anchored.'
+flextech-2-chair: 'FlexTech-2 continues the FlexTech chair on human systems integration after its first five-year term. It is led by ESTIA in Bidart with IRIT, the Toulouse computer-science institute of Université de Toulouse where Philippe Palanque''s interactive critical systems group sits, and its founding industrial members are SNCF and Ingenuity i/o, the Toulouse company behind the Ingescape platform used for human-in-the-loop prototyping. Guy André Boy, now professor emeritus, is the chair''s advisor; the acting holder is a professor attached to ESTIA and IRIT.
+
+
+  The programme keeps the first chair''s agenda — flexibility rather than rigid automation, tangibility, PRODEC scenario-based design, digital twins of sociotechnical systems, human–AI teaming — and adds railway operations through SNCF. The entry is kept at medium confidence: the 2025 chair report names the partners and the 2025–2030 horizon, but the funding and the holder''s name were not retrievable when this was written.'
+flextech-chair: 'The FlexTech chair was a joint research and teaching chair of CentraleSupélec (Université Paris-Saclay) and ESTIA (Bidart, Basque Country), launched in September 2019 and held by Guy André Boy until 2024. Its subject was human systems integration in complex systems with increasing autonomy — the move "from HighTech to FlexTech": rather than automating and then patching the human back in, design flexibility, tangibility and the organisation into the system from the start, with human-in-the-loop simulation and digital twins as the working method.
+
+
+  Industrial partners brought the use cases: remote operations and digital twins for diagnosis (TotalEnergies), fleets of drones and mission systems (CS Group, Armée de l''Air et de l''Espace), helicopter-engine maintenance and support (Safran Helicopter Engines), avionics and mission systems (Thales), and the Ingescape interactive-systems platform (Ingenuity i/o) used to prototype the human–machine teaming scenarios. Theses were co-directed with Marija Jankovic at CentraleSupélec and co-advised at ESTIA and in the partner companies. The chair trained eight PhD students and some five hundred master-level students, produced about seventy papers, the PRODEC method, the Reality Anchor digital-twin methodology and the 2020 HSI reference book, and hosted the first INCOSE HSI conference in Biarritz. It closed with a synthesis lecture at ESTIA in February 2025; a FlexTech-2 chair led by ESTIA with IRIT (Toulouse), SNCF and Ingenuity i/o takes over.'
+hci-aero: 'HCI-Aero was the conference series that Guy André Boy launched in Toulouse in 1986 — first as "Human-Machine Interaction and Artificial Intelligence in Aerospace", renamed HCI-Aero in 1998 — and ran every two years, alternating between Europe and North America, in cooperation with ACM SIGCHI, with EURISCO organising the European editions and AAAI then ACM publishing the proceedings. Its subject was the cockpit and the control room at the moment automation and early AI entered them: authority sharing between crew and automation, cognitive function analysis, intelligent assistants, air-traffic management. Editions were held in Toulouse (1986, 2000), Montréal (1998), Cambridge MA (2002), Seattle (2006), Cape Canaveral (2010), Brussels (2012), Santa Clara (2014) and Paris (2016).
+
+
+  No edition has been held since Paris 2016; the community migrated to the INCOSE HSI conference series that Boy founded in 2019. The entry is kept, concluded, so that the period filter shows the thirty years of aerospace human–computer interaction work that preceded today''s human–AI teaming programmes.'
+human-systems-integration: 'Human systems integration (HSI), as framed by Guy André Boy, is the discipline that integrates technology, organizations and people (the TOP model) over the whole life cycle of a sociotechnical system — design, development, validation, operation, maintenance and dismantling. It grew out of cognitive engineering and human-centered design and was made a full discipline through the INCOSE HSI Working Group (chaired by Boy 2014–2024) and the FlexTech chair. Its working concepts are flexibility rather than rigid automation, tangibility (a virtual model must become physically and cognitively graspable), sociotechnical maturity, and the distinction between procedural and declarative knowledge; the PRODEC method (2022) turns them into an agile, scenario-based design loop with human-in-the-loop simulation and digital twins.
+
+
+  Two recent papers anchor the framework for human–AI collaboration. The 2023 epistemological approach (Technology in Society) argues for a consistent terminology and ontology of HSI — complexity, context, procedural versus declarative knowledge, maturity. Systemic cognition (Systems, 2026) puts context at the centre: an STS is understood incrementally through conceptual models built in context, so that emerging behaviours discovered in operation are integrated back into the system. For a map of human–AI decision-making in critical systems, HSI is the systems-engineering frame in which oversight, mixed initiative and human–AI teaming are negotiated as function allocation and authority, not bolted on as interfaces.'
+incose-hsi-community: 'The INCOSE Human Systems Integration Working Group is the standing community that turned human systems integration into a recognised systems-engineering practice: its charter is to ensure that the human elements of a system are addressed throughout the engineering life cycle, and its products include the HSI Primer (2023), the HSI section of the INCOSE Systems Engineering Handbook (5th edition, aligned with ISO/IEC/IEEE 15288:2023) and the SEBoK article on HSI. Guy André Boy chaired it from 2014 to 2024 and founded its conference and workshop series, organised in cooperation with ACM SIGCHI and the International Ergonomics Association, which also runs an HSI technical committee.
+
+
+  The series began in Europe — HSI2019 in Biarritz, hosted with ESTIA, is why the entry is anchored there — and alternates conferences (2019, 2021 virtual, 2024 Jeju) with workshops (2020, 2022 Torrance, 2023 Seattle, 2025 virtual); a Toulouse edition is planned for 2026 and a joint conference with the IEA world congress for 2027. For the European human–AI collaboration landscape it is the venue where FlexTech, ESTIA, CentraleSupélec, ETH Zurich, Airbus, Thales and Dassault contributions on human–AI teaming, authority and tangibility are presented and reviewed.'
+ingenuity-io: 'Ingenuity i/o is a Toulouse software company founded in 2012 by Stéphane Valès, an ENAC-trained air-traffic and computer engineer from the French aeronautical human-computer interaction community. Its product, Ingescape, is a model-based framework for distributed interactive software: agents, displays, 2D/3D interaction devices and simulators are described and orchestrated without a central broker, so that a control position, a cockpit or a supervision room can be prototyped and re-wired quickly, and the same models feed verification and human-factors evaluation.
+
+
+  It sits on this map because it is the tooling partner of the human systems integration community: Ingescape was the platform on which the FlexTech chair ran its human-in-the-loop simulations and PRODEC scenarios, it was taught at the 2024 spring school on human–AI teaming, and the company is a founding industrial member of the FlexTech-2 chair led by ESTIA and IRIT.'
+lamih-uphf: 'LAMIH is the multidisciplinary laboratory of the Université Polytechnique Hauts-de-France in Valenciennes — automatic control, mechanics, computer science and human sciences — and the historical home of the French school of human–machine cooperation. Patrick Millot''s Human–Machine Systems group, created in 1987, formalised cooperation between a human operator and an automated assistant as a problem of shared supervision, task allocation and common work space, with applications to air-traffic control, road traffic and metro or train supervision; Frédéric Vanderhaegen extended it to dissonance, resilience and human reliability, and the group has long been a pillar of the IFAC human–machine systems community.
+
+
+  Its link with Guy André Boy is a shared cognitive-engineering lineage rather than a common institution: Millot wrote the supervision and cooperation chapter of Boy''s Ingénierie cognitive (2003) and co-authored with him "Human-machine cooperation: a solution for life-critical systems?" (Work, 2012). Current work by Marie-Pierre Pacaux-Lemoine and colleagues moves the same questions to remote and autonomous train driving, human–robot teaming and human–cyber-physical-system cooperation in manufacturing — operator-in-the-loop decision-making in rail and industry, the core of this map.'
+safran-helicopter-engines: 'Safran Helicopter Engines, headquartered at Bordes near Pau, is the world''s leading helicopter-engine maker and runs its main production and R&D site there, transformed since 2010 under a "factory of the future" programme. Its place on this map comes from a human systems integration project run with the FlexTech chair: SOMA, a multi-agent, self-learning digital twin designed to support collaborative decision-making in helicopter-engine maintenance. Rather than an automated prescriptive-maintenance engine, SOMA coordinates human and artificial agents through stigmergy — each leaves traces the others use — so that maintenance professionals keep the decision while the twin accumulates and redistributes the knowledge.
+
+
+  The work was a CIFRE thesis at ESTIA supervised within the chair (INCOSE International Symposium 2022, Springer 2025), prototyped and tested with the Bordes maintenance teams; Safran Helicopter Engines is also a historic partner of the ESTIA foundation. It illustrates HSI applied to industrial maintenance: a digital twin built as a partner in a decision, with tangibility and function allocation designed in from the start.'
