+description: |
+  The FlexTech chair was a joint research and teaching chair of CentraleSupélec
+  (Université Paris-Saclay) and ESTIA (Bidart, Basque Country), launched in September 2019
+  and held by Guy André Boy until 2024. Its subject was human systems integration in
+  complex systems with increasing autonomy — the move "from HighTech to FlexTech": rather
+  than automating and then patching the human back in, design flexibility, tangibility and
+  the organisation into the system from the start, with human-in-the-loop simulation and
+  digital twins as the working method.
+
+  Industrial partners brought the use cases: remote operations and digital twins for
+  diagnosis (TotalEnergies), fleets of drones and mission systems (CS Group, Armée de l'Air
+  et de l'Espace), helicopter-engine maintenance and support (Safran Helicopter Engines),
+  avionics and mission systems (Thales), and the Ingescape interactive-systems platform
+  (Ingenuity i/o) used to prototype the human–machine teaming scenarios. Theses were
+  co-directed with Marija Jankovic at CentraleSupélec and co-advised at ESTIA and in the
+  partner companies. The chair trained eight PhD students and some five hundred
+  master-level students, produced about seventy papers, the PRODEC method, the Reality
+  Anchor digital-twin methodology and the 2020 HSI reference book, and hosted the first
+  INCOSE HSI conference in Biarritz.
+  It closed with a synthesis lecture at ESTIA in February 2025; a FlexTech-2 chair led by
+  ESTIA with IRIT (Toulouse), SNCF and Ingenuity i/o takes over.
