+description: |
+  The Académie de l'air et de l'espace, founded in Toulouse in 1983, is the French learned
+  society of aeronautics and space. Among its working themes the relation between crews,
+  automation and now artificial intelligence recurs: Dossier 37 (2013) on dealing with
+  unforeseen situations in flight, drawn from a colloquium with airline pilots on facing the
+  unexpected; Dossier 42 (2018) on a more automated, interconnected aviation by 2050 and its
+  companion study on transport-aircraft automation; and an international colloquium in
+  November 2025 on AI applications in aeronautics, space and defence, whose synthesis
+  stresses that the human–machine interface problems predate AI and multiply with it.
+
+  Guy André Boy has been a fellow of the academy since 2006 and one of its voices on human
+  systems integration and cognitive engineering — the 2020 note "IA-t-il un pilote dans
+  l'avion ?" and his contribution to the 2025 colloquium carry the HSI position into the
+  French aerospace establishment. The academy sits in the ecosystem tier: it does not run
+  research, but it frames how the aerospace community in Toulouse talks about authority,
+  automation and AI in the cockpit.
