+description: |
+  EURISCO (Institut européen de sciences et d'ingénierie cognitives) was the joint
+  cognitive-engineering research institute of Airbus and Thomson-CSF/Thales, founded in
+  Toulouse in 1992 and run until 2008 by Guy André Boy, who had come from ONERA's
+  cognitive ergonomics group and NASA Ames, with the test pilot Jean Pinet as co-founder
+  and president in the 1990s. Its multidisciplinary staff — cognitive and social
+  scientists, computer scientists, engineers, human-factors specialists — worked with a
+  network of universities and engineering schools, and the institute traded as EURISCO
+  International in its last years. It brought cognitive science into cockpit and
+  air-traffic automation design at the moment glass cockpits and fly-by-wire raised the
+  question of authority between crew and automation: cognitive function analysis,
+  situation awareness and workload in highly automated cockpits, human-centred
+  automation, procedure design and, later, authority sharing in future air-traffic
+  management. It is the industrial origin of cognitive function analysis and of the
+  Orchestra model, and it ran the HCI-Aero conference series with ACM SIGCHI.
+
+  The institute closed in 2008; its line of work moved with Boy to the Human-Centered
+  Design Institute at Florida Tech and returned to Europe with the FlexTech chair in
+  2019. It is kept on the map as a concluded entry so that the period filter shows where
+  the French cognitive-engineering community of the 1990s and 2000s was anchored.
