+description: |
+  LAMIH is the multidisciplinary laboratory of the Université Polytechnique Hauts-de-France
+  in Valenciennes — automatic control, mechanics, computer science and human sciences —
+  and the historical home of the French school of human–machine cooperation. Patrick
+  Millot's Human–Machine Systems group, created in 1987, formalised cooperation between a
+  human operator and an automated assistant as a problem of shared supervision, task
+  allocation and common work space, with applications to air-traffic control, road traffic
+  and metro or train supervision; Frédéric Vanderhaegen extended it to dissonance,
+  resilience and human reliability, and the group has long been a pillar of the IFAC
+  human–machine systems community.
+
+  Its link with Guy André Boy is a shared cognitive-engineering lineage rather than a
+  common institution: Millot wrote the supervision and cooperation chapter of Boy's
+  Ingénierie cognitive (2003) and co-authored with him "Human-machine cooperation: a
+  solution for life-critical systems?" (Work, 2012). Current work by Marie-Pierre
+  Pacaux-Lemoine and colleagues moves the same questions to remote and autonomous train
+  driving, human–robot teaming and human–cyber-physical-system cooperation in
+  manufacturing — operator-in-the-loop decision-making in rail and industry, the core of
+  this map.
